{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>I am too small for all the kindnesses.</b> It appears that Jacob mentioned before HaShem, blessed be He, two promises, as Rashi explained, and said: just as You fulfilled for me one promise, so may You fulfill for me the second as well. For the first promise was concerning protection, as it is written: ״וּשְׁמַרְתִּיךָ בְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר תֵּלֵךְ וַהֲשִׁבֹתִיךָ אֶל הָאֲדָמָה הַזֹּאת״ (Genesis 28:15). And after he had already been promised protection and that He would return him to the land of his birth — if so, what need was there to promise him a second time in Beth Laban: ״שׁוּב אֶל אֶרֶץ מוֹלַדְתֶּךָ וְאֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ״ (Genesis 31:3)? Rather, surely this promise was not said concerning protection from all evil, turning away from evil — I mean, to remove evil from him — but rather this promise was about the doing of good, that is, to bestow good upon him. Therefore he said here, in place of ״וְאֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ,״ ״וְאֵיטִיבָה עִמָּךְ,״ and this is the explanation of ״וְאֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ.״" "Now, regarding the matter of bestowing good, it should have said ״וְאֵיטִיבָה לָךְ,״ but it used ״עִמָּךְ״ to indicate that in the matter of receiving good things, the Master must be with him in his labor. For a person must act through natural means, doing all that he finds within his power to do, and the Holy One, blessed be He, will complete it through him and add to him an abundance of blessing, as explained above in connection with the verse ״וַיְהִי לִי שׁוֹר וַחֲמוֹר״ (Genesis 32:6). And this is what it says: ״וְאֵיטִיבָה עִמָּךְ״ (Genesis 32:13). And this is the second promise — that He promised to send the blessing into his storehouses. And concerning this promise he said, by way of what appears already to have been fulfilled, ״קָטֹנְתִּי מִכֹּל הַחֲסָדִים״ (Genesis 32:11), for Jacob was small and his strength too feeble to hang all the acquisition of his wealth and property on his own strength and the might of his hand; rather, surely the kindness of El is constant — it was He who made this wealth for him." "And the proof for this: ״כִּי בְמַקְלִי עָבַרְתִּי אֶת הַיַּרְדֵּן הַזֶּה״ (Genesis 32:11) — and I was empty of everything, for there was nothing in my hand except my staff — ״וְעַתָּה הָיִיתִי לִשְׁנֵי מַחֲנוֹת.״ In saying ״וְעַתָּה,״ he indicated that it seemed to him as though he had made all this wealth just now, in the blink of an eye. For fourteen years he served for two daughters and six years for flocks; and when Laban said to him, ״נָקְבָה עָלַי שְׂכָרְךָ וְאֶתֵּנָה״ (Genesis 30:28), in a short time he made this great wealth and property — which according to nature it would not have been possible to acquire such a vast fortune in so short a time, were it not that the hand of HaShem did this. And even though Jacob sent to Esav with the very opposite intent, as explained above regarding the verse ״וַיְהִי לִי שׁוֹר וַחֲמוֹר״ (Genesis 32:6), and denied him out of fear, so as not to let a falsehood proceed from his mouth by telling him that he had toiled over his flocks for the entire span of twenty years — but the truth is not so, for far be it to say that Jacob would say ״כֹּחִי וְעֹצֶם יָדִי עָשָׂה לִי אֶת הַחַיִל הַזֶּה״ (Deuteronomy 8:17). And this is what he said: ״וְעַתָּה הָיִיתִי לִשְׁנֵי מַחֲנוֹת״ — in the blink of an eye. For had he owned flocks and cattle already when he crossed the Jordan, I would say that his possessions grew by natural means. But now that he acknowledged that with his staff alone he crossed the Jordan — if so, when was all this wealth made, something from nothing? Surely it was from HaShem that this came. Therefore he asked: just as You fulfilled for me this second promise, so may You fulfill for me also the first, which You promised me concerning protection — ״הַצִּילֵנִי נָא מִיַּד אָחִי מִיַּד עֵשָׂו כִּי יָרֵא אָנֹכִי אֹתוֹ״ (Genesis 32:12). And the word ״אֹתוֹ״ is superfluous; rather, he alluded to that sin which he committed in that he flattered the wicked man and turned holy into profane, and ״אֹתוֹ״ refers to the sin. And there are those who say that for himself he was not afraid, for he had already been promised protection; only he feared lest he come and ״וְהִכַּנִי אֵם עַל בָּנִים״ (Genesis 32:12), for they had not been included in the promise of protection."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The citation of ״וְאֵיטִיבָה עִמָּךְ״ appears to be drawn from Jacob's paraphrase of the divine promise later in the same chapter (Genesis 32:13), understood here as a gloss on the original ״וְאֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ״ of Genesis 31:3."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:49.659745306Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}